This is a very common enterprise business rule. The core financial transaction (Oracle) is the source of truth, and the analytics sync (HDFS) is secondary. If HDFS goes down, it should **never** block a financial approval. The user should see "Accepted" immediately, and the backend should secretly retry the HDFS sync in the background later.
We will remove the PROCESSING status logic we just added and revert the HDFS failure so that it silently queues for retry instead of rolling back the Oracle transaction.
Here are the **3 exact replacements** to make in your **JournalRequestServiceImpl.java**.
### 1. Update executeApprovalInTransaction (The HDFS Block)
*Find the hdfsSyncService block (around line 630). We are removing the exception throw so that if HDFS fails, Oracle stays committed, the batch becomes ACCEPTED, and the user gets a success notification!*
**Replace the HDFS try-catch block with this:**
```java
            try {
                hdfsSyncService.syncToDataLake(syncData);
            } catch (Exception hdfsEx) {
                // Silently catch the HDFS error. DO NOT roll back Oracle.
                log.error("HDFS_SYNC_FAIL | Batch: {} | Error: {} | Status: Background recovery queued", batchId, hdfsEx.getClass().getSimpleName());
                
                // Put it in the retry queue so the HdfsRecoveryService picks it up later
                jdbcTemplate.update("INSERT INTO HDFS_SYNC_RETRY_QUEUE (BATCH_ID, STATUS) VALUES (?, 'PENDING')", batchId);
            }

            // The user will see 100% success even if HDFS failed!
            progressService.sendProgress(batchId, executorId, 100, "COMPLETED", "Batch Approved Successfully");
            notificationWriterService.createBatchApprovedNotification(batchId, creatorId, executorId);

```
### 2. Update getPendingBatchSummaries
*We are removing the PROCESSING mapping. If a batch is QUEUED in the database, the user will just see it as PENDING.*
**Replace the entire method with:**
```java
    @Override
    public List<Map<String, Object>> getPendingBatchSummaries() {
        // Query QUEUED and ERROR to catch any locked/legacy records so they don't vanish
        String sql = "SELECT BATCH_ID, CREATOR_ID, REQ_DATE, BATCH_REMARKS, TOTAL_ROWS, TOTAL_DEBIT, TOTAL_CREDIT, BATCH_STATUS, EXECUTOR_REMARKS " +
                "FROM JOURNAL_BATCH_MASTER WHERE BATCH_STATUS IN ('PENDING', 'QUEUED', 'ERROR') ORDER BY REQ_DATE DESC";

        return jdbcTemplate.query(sql, (rs, rowNum) -> {
            Map<String, Object> map = new HashMap<>();
            map.put("batchId", rs.getString("BATCH_ID"));
            map.put("creatorId", rs.getString("CREATOR_ID"));
            map.put("requestDate", rs.getTimestamp("REQ_DATE"));
            map.put("commonBatchRemarks", rs.getString("BATCH_REMARKS"));
            map.put("requestCount", rs.getLong("TOTAL_ROWS"));
            map.put("totalDebit", rs.getBigDecimal("TOTAL_DEBIT"));
            map.put("totalCredit", rs.getBigDecimal("TOTAL_CREDIT"));
            
            // Strictly force the frontend to only see "PENDING"
            map.put("requestStatus", "PENDING");
            
            // Completely hide any error messages from the frontend UI
            map.put("errorMessage", null);
            
            return map;
        });
    }

```
### 3. Update getAllBatchSummaries
*Apply the exact same strict PENDING mapping rule to the History screen, stripping out the PROCESSING status.*
**Replace the entire method with:**
```java
    @Override
    public List<Map<String, Object>> getAllBatchSummaries() {
        String sql = "SELECT BATCH_ID, CREATOR_ID, REQ_DATE, BATCH_REMARKS, TOTAL_ROWS, TOTAL_DEBIT, TOTAL_CREDIT, BATCH_STATUS, EXECUTOR_ID, EXECUTOR_REMARKS " +
                "FROM JOURNAL_BATCH_MASTER ORDER BY REQ_DATE DESC FETCH FIRST 100 ROWS ONLY";

        return jdbcTemplate.query(sql, (rs, rowNum) -> {
            Map<String, Object> map = new HashMap<>();
            map.put("batchId", rs.getString("BATCH_ID"));
            map.put("creatorId", rs.getString("CREATOR_ID"));
            map.put("requestDate", rs.getTimestamp("REQ_DATE"));
            map.put("commonBatchRemarks", rs.getString("BATCH_REMARKS"));
            map.put("requestCount", rs.getLong("TOTAL_ROWS"));
            map.put("totalDebit", rs.getBigDecimal("TOTAL_DEBIT"));
            map.put("totalCredit", rs.getBigDecimal("TOTAL_CREDIT"));
            
            // Map QUEUED and legacy ERROR rows back to PENDING for the UI
            String dbStatus = rs.getString("BATCH_STATUS");
            if ("QUEUED".equals(dbStatus) || "ERROR".equals(dbStatus)) {
                map.put("requestStatus", "PENDING");
                map.put("executorRemarks", null); // Wipe error remarks
            } else {
                map.put("requestStatus", dbStatus);
                map.put("executorRemarks", rs.getString("EXECUTOR_REMARKS"));
            }
            
            map.put("executorId", rs.getString("EXECUTOR_ID"));
            return map;
        });
    }

```
With these changes:
 1. The user will **never** know if the Data Lake (HDFS) crashes. They will instantly get an "Approval Successful" message because Oracle saved their data perfectly.
 2. The UI is completely simplified back to just PENDING, ACCEPTED, and REJECTED.
